# Bootstrapping pkgsrc on OpenVMS

This port targets VSI OpenVMS x86-64 with VSI C and GNV 3.0-2F or newer.
It currently bootstraps a static, C-only pkgsrc environment.  C++, parallel
bmake jobs, general shared-image support, and general DECwindows/X11 buildlink
support are follow-up work.  Packages with native OpenVMS build systems can
already use installed shareable images; `vim-motif` does this for DECwindows.

## Prerequisites

Use an ODS-5 volume with extended filenames and hard links enabled.  Keep the
pkgsrc source, work directory, package prefix, database, and temporary files on
that volume; a bootstrap requires substantially more space than the OpenVMS
system disk normally provides.

The account also needs enough paging-file quota for Autoconf's generated
scripts and the full `pkg_install` build.  A `PGFLQUOTA` of 12,000,000
pagelets is known to work for a clean bootstrap and subsequent package builds.
An administrator can set it with `MCR AUTHORIZE MODIFY user
/PGFLQUOTA=12000000`; start a new login session after changing the UAF record.
The system must have enough page-file capacity to satisfy that quota.  The
reference VM uses an 8 GiB secondary page file, installed at boot from
`SYS$MANAGER:SYPAGSWPFILES.COM`.

Increase the build account's subprocess quota as well.  A pkgsrc recipe nests
the framework make, the package make, its shell wrapper, and compiler helper
processes; the usual `PRCLM` value of 8 is too small.  A value of 100 is known
to work and can be set with `MCR AUTHORIZE MODIFY user /PRCLM=100`.  This also
requires a new login session.

Before starting GNV, create a scratch directory on the same ODS-5 volume as
the pkgsrc work directories.  GNV patch uses `SYS$SCRATCH` for replacement
files, and OpenVMS cannot rename those files across volumes.

```
$ CREATE/DIRECTORY disk:[user.pkgsrc-tmp]
$ DEFINE/JOB SYS$SCRATCH disk:[user.pkgsrc-tmp]
```

Then enable the extended C RTL pathname behavior in the DCL process that will
run the bootstrap:

```
$ DEFINE/JOB DECC$UNIX_LEVEL 30
$ DEFINE/JOB DECC$POSIX_COMPLIANT_PATHNAMES ENABLE
$ DEFINE/JOB DECC$UNIX_PATH_BEFORE_LOGNAME ENABLE
$ DEFINE/JOB DECC$STREAM_PIPE ENABLE
$ SET PROCESS/PARSE_STYLE=EXTENDED
$ BASH
```

Use the job logical table: GNV emulates some `exec` operations with spawned
processes that do not retain process-table logical names.  The level-30
setting is significant for builds: it enables Unix filename,
glob, permission, and sharing behavior recommended by VSI for Bash and GNV.
In particular, compiler probe images are replaced instead of accumulating as
OpenVMS file versions, and shell wildcard cleanup works as Autoconf expects.
Stream pipes are also required for configure scripts: with legacy record pipes,
Bash can wait minutes for EOF after a completed command substitution.  VSI's
GNV release notes warn that stream pipes can hang the specific `bash dcl git`
combination, so use this job only for pkgsrc builds and do not run Git through
the GNV `dcl` wrapper in it.  This setting must be a job logical before Bash is
started; exporting a Unix environment variable with the same name does not
enable the C RTL feature.  pkgsrc checks it before starting a package's
configure phase and reports a direct error when it is missing.

Run the bootstrap explicitly with Bash.  Direct shebang execution and GNV
Bash's POSIX mode are not reliable on this release.

```
bash$ cd /path/to/pkgsrc
bash$ bash ./bootstrap/bootstrap \
    --unprivileged \
    --cwrappers no \
    --make-jobs 1 \
    --prefix /path/to/pkg \
    --workdir /path/to/pkgsrc-bootstrap
```

Do not relocate the pkgsrc checkout after bootstrapping.  The generated
configuration and compiler wrapper refer to files in that checkout.

## Current GNV workarounds

- Autoconf is prevented from enabling the broken GNV Bash POSIX mode.
- Autoconf executable probes treat regular files as runnable because GNV does
  not expose OpenVMS images with Unix execute bits.
- Autoconf's false-negative probe for the OpenVMS `vfork` macro is cached.
- Autoconf's shell re-exec path is suppressed because GNV emulates `exec` by
  spawning and waiting, allowing code after a nominally successful `exec` to
  run and report a false failure.
- Libtool is told to use Bash's XSI parameter expansions instead of its
  sed-pipeline fallback, which is unreliable under the GNV pipe emulation.
- bmake metadata mode is disabled.
- bmake captures `!=` command output through regular files rather than a
  `vfork`/pipe sequence, which is not valid for the OpenVMS C RTL emulation.
- bmake compatibility mode runs recipes through Bash so scripts do not depend
  on unavailable Unix execute-bit and `execvp` fallback semantics.
- The pkgsrc compiler wrapper invokes the native frontend directly and adds a
  harmless C source to object-only links.
- Package builds stay out of bmake's pipe-based job mode, even when the
  bootstrap passes `MAKE_JOBS=1`.
- libnbcompat's FTS implementation always uses full-path traversal because
  OpenVMS cannot open directories as file descriptors and has no `fchdir()`.
- libarchive is configured out of tree with dependency tracking disabled;
  Automake's depfile pass otherwise creates a very slow nested process chain.
- The bootstrap builds only libarchive's static library target, avoiding test
  inventory rules that use GNU make automatic variables unsupported by bmake.
- The libarchive build reuses the requested bootstrap `CFLAGS`; VSI's gcc
  frontend otherwise accepts Autoconf probes for unsupported GNU warning and
  section flags but only diagnoses them during every real compilation.
- pkgsrc uses static libraries, single-job builds, no stripping, and no
  `ranlib` during the initial port.

## Platform identity

Keep `OPSYS` as `OpenVMS`, matching the product name and `uname -s`.  GNU
configuration names use the established lower-case operating-system component
and historical vendor component, so the x86-64 canonical triplet is
`x86_64-dec-vms`.  Using `x86_64-vsi-vms`, or globally replacing `OpenVMS`
with `VMS` in pkgsrc conditionals, would make this port disagree with GNU
config and existing package tests.

## Validated packages

The following end-user packages have clean binary packages and runtime tests
on the reference VSI OpenVMS x86-64 system:

- `ncurses-6.6`: headers, static libraries, utilities, and the terminfo
  database; both `tic`/`infocmp` and a linked `getch()` test are validated.
- `lynx-2.9.2nb8`: curses UI, local-file rendering, and HTTP retrieval are
  validated.  The initial port deliberately disables NLS, TLS, and IPv6.
- `vim-share-9.2.1025` and `vim-motif-9.2.1025`: runtime files, `xxd`, the
  native MMS-built Huge Vim image, and its DECwindows/Motif X11 GUI are
  validated.  This is release tag `v9.2.1025`, not the moving master branch.

Build the packages serially.  Vim's runtime package must precede its GUI
package:

```
bash$ cd devel/ncurses && bmake clean package && bmake install
bash$ cd ../../www/lynx && bmake clean package && bmake install
bash$ cd ../../editors/vim-share && bmake clean package && bmake install
bash$ cd ../vim-motif && bmake clean package && bmake install
```

The Vim package installs both `bin/vim` for GNV shells and `bin/vim.exe` for
native DCL.  For example, after translating the Unix prefix to its native VMS
directory syntax:

```
$ MCR disk:[user.pkg.bin]vim --version
```

The image contains fallback locations for `VIM` and `VIMRUNTIME`.  A login or
CDE launcher that already defines those logical names for another Vim version
must override them for the pkgsrc image, for example:

```
$ DEFINE/PROCESS VIM disk:[user.pkg.share.vim]
$ DEFINE/PROCESS VIMRUNTIME disk:[user.pkg.share.vim.vim92]
$ DEFINE/USER SYS$INPUT NLA0:
$ MCR disk:[user.pkg.bin]vim -g
```

For CDE Application Manager, put a procedure such as this under `SYS$LOGIN`:

```
$ SET NOON
$ SET DISPLAY/CREATE/TRANSPORT=TCPIP/NODE=192.0.2.10/SERVER=1/SCREEN=0
$ DEFINE/PROCESS DISPLAY "192.0.2.10:1.0"
$ DEFINE/PROCESS VIM disk:[user.pkg.share.vim]
$ DEFINE/PROCESS VIMRUNTIME disk:[user.pkg.share.vim.vim92]
$ DEFINE/USER SYS$INPUT NLA0:
$ MCR disk:[user.pkg.bin]vim -g
$ EXIT $STATUS
```

Replace the example X server address, display number, and installation disk.
Run the image directly: CDE already owns the action process, while an extra
DCL `SPAWN/NOWAIT` can terminate with its short-lived parent before the GUI is
mapped.

Install an action definition like the following as
`SYS$LOGIN:[.DT.TYPES]VIM92.DT`:

```
ACTION vim92
{
        LABEL           Vim 9.2.1025
        ARG_COUNT       0
        ICON            Dxeve
        TYPE            COMMAND
        WINDOW_TYPE     NO_STDIO
        EXEC_STRING     $ @SYS$LOGIN:VIM92_CDE.COM
        DESCRIPTION     Vim graphical Motif editor for OpenVMS.
}
```

Finally, add an executable file named `VIM92` to the appropriate
`SYS$LOGIN:[.DT.APPMANAGER...]` folder so Application Manager exposes the
action.  A newly started CDE session loads the per-user action database.
